WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.175
At this stage of the course,

2
00:00:02.175 --> 00:00:03.450
you spend some time exploring

3
00:00:03.450 --> 00:00:05.415
the relational model
for databases.

4
00:00:05.415 --> 00:00:07.410
However, it's crucial
that you have

5
00:00:07.410 --> 00:00:08.760
a proper understanding of how

6
00:00:08.760 --> 00:00:10.410
the relational model influences

7
00:00:10.410 --> 00:00:12.810
the design and
structure of a database

8
00:00:12.810 --> 00:00:16.575
and how it helps to build
relationships between tables.

9
00:00:16.575 --> 00:00:19.500
Once you understand how your
database is structured,

10
00:00:19.500 --> 00:00:21.090
then you can determine how best

11
00:00:21.090 --> 00:00:22.980
to extract information from it.

12
00:00:22.980 --> 00:00:24.720
Over the next few minutes,

13
00:00:24.720 --> 00:00:26.130
you'll learn how to outline

14
00:00:26.130 --> 00:00:27.930
the basics of the
relational model,

15
00:00:27.930 --> 00:00:30.975
identify the different
relationships between tables,

16
00:00:30.975 --> 00:00:34.050
and explain the basics
of an ER-Diagram.

17
00:00:34.050 --> 00:00:36.630
To understand how
the relational model

18
00:00:36.630 --> 00:00:38.265
influences our databases,

19
00:00:38.265 --> 00:00:39.600
let's take the examples of

20
00:00:39.600 --> 00:00:41.935
two tables from a
college database.

21
00:00:41.935 --> 00:00:44.960
The first table shows a
list of students along

22
00:00:44.960 --> 00:00:46.040
with their assigned student

23
00:00:46.040 --> 00:00:48.250
and course
identification numbers.

24
00:00:48.250 --> 00:00:51.050
The second table list
courses that students can

25
00:00:51.050 --> 00:00:52.700
study along with the ID

26
00:00:52.700 --> 00:00:54.695
for each course and
its department.

27
00:00:54.695 --> 00:00:56.915
The big question in this example

28
00:00:56.915 --> 00:00:59.530
is which student is
studying what course?

29
00:00:59.530 --> 00:01:03.400
Is each student studying
one or multiple courses?

30
00:01:03.400 --> 00:01:05.480
These are basic
examples of why it's

31
00:01:05.480 --> 00:01:08.320
important to structure and
connect tables correctly.

32
00:01:08.320 --> 00:01:10.835
There are three types of
relationships between

33
00:01:10.835 --> 00:01:13.255
any two tables in a
relational database,

34
00:01:13.255 --> 00:01:17.705
one-to-many, one-to-one,
and many-to-many.

35
00:01:17.705 --> 00:01:19.685
Let's begin with an exploration

36
00:01:19.685 --> 00:01:21.710
of the one-to-many relationship.

37
00:01:21.710 --> 00:01:23.990
In a one-to-many relationship,

38
00:01:23.990 --> 00:01:26.630
a record of data in
a row of one table

39
00:01:26.630 --> 00:01:28.145
is linked to multiple records

40
00:01:28.145 --> 00:01:30.070
in different rows of another.

41
00:01:30.070 --> 00:01:33.020
The student table, a
student with the idea of

42
00:01:33.020 --> 00:01:36.635
one is enrolled in two
courses on the course table.

43
00:01:36.635 --> 00:01:38.690
One-to-many relationship can be

44
00:01:38.690 --> 00:01:41.080
drawn between these tables.

45
00:01:41.080 --> 00:01:43.670
This relationship can
also be illustrated in

46
00:01:43.670 --> 00:01:47.250
a basic entity relationship
diagram, or ERD.

47
00:01:47.250 --> 00:01:49.010
A student is enrolled in

48
00:01:49.010 --> 00:01:52.135
many courses using
shapes and symbols.

49
00:01:52.135 --> 00:01:54.440
The diagram depicts
the two entities,

50
00:01:54.440 --> 00:01:55.760
student and course,

51
00:01:55.760 --> 00:01:57.530
in rectangle shapes with

52
00:01:57.530 --> 00:02:01.040
enrolled to describe the
relationship in a diamond shape.

53
00:02:01.040 --> 00:02:05.530
Many is depicted using the
Crow's foot notation symbol.

54
00:02:05.530 --> 00:02:08.360
The relationship can also
be illustrated using

55
00:02:08.360 --> 00:02:12.145
a more complex ER-Diagram
that depicts keys.

56
00:02:12.145 --> 00:02:16.895
Course ID in the student
table is a foreign key or FK.

57
00:02:16.895 --> 00:02:19.520
This references
the primary key or

58
00:02:19.520 --> 00:02:23.905
PK course ID column that
exists in the course table.

59
00:02:23.905 --> 00:02:27.770
Next, let's take a look at
one-to-one relationships.

60
00:02:27.770 --> 00:02:31.070
In one-to-one relationships,
one single record of

61
00:02:31.070 --> 00:02:32.720
one table is associated with

62
00:02:32.720 --> 00:02:34.895
one single record
of another table.

63
00:02:34.895 --> 00:02:37.085
To demonstrate
this relationship,

64
00:02:37.085 --> 00:02:39.050
I'll use two new tables.

65
00:02:39.050 --> 00:02:41.390
One that [inaudible]
lines key information

66
00:02:41.390 --> 00:02:43.835
about the staff in each
college department.

67
00:02:43.835 --> 00:02:46.910
The other is the department
location table that records

68
00:02:46.910 --> 00:02:50.675
key data where the location
of each department on campus.

69
00:02:50.675 --> 00:02:53.570
In this instance, each
department head is

70
00:02:53.570 --> 00:02:56.405
in one department building
on the college campus.

71
00:02:56.405 --> 00:02:57.890
Each staff member from

72
00:02:57.890 --> 00:02:59.660
the department staff table is

73
00:02:59.660 --> 00:03:02.870
associated with one record
from the department table.

74
00:03:02.870 --> 00:03:05.750
These relationships can
also be depicted in

75
00:03:05.750 --> 00:03:07.460
an ER diagram as

76
00:03:07.460 --> 00:03:10.240
one department head
leads one department.

77
00:03:10.240 --> 00:03:13.925
Finally, there's also
many-to-many relationships.

78
00:03:13.925 --> 00:03:16.670
This type of relationship
associates one record of

79
00:03:16.670 --> 00:03:19.865
one table with multiple
records of another table.

80
00:03:19.865 --> 00:03:22.940
The same relationship also
works in the other direction.

81
00:03:22.940 --> 00:03:26.195
In this example, the
student, Maurice Doyle,

82
00:03:26.195 --> 00:03:28.835
is undertaking two
research projects and

83
00:03:28.835 --> 00:03:31.925
each project is supervised
by different staff member.

84
00:03:31.925 --> 00:03:35.150
Likewise, one staff
member can supervise or

85
00:03:35.150 --> 00:03:37.145
collaborate with
multiple students

86
00:03:37.145 --> 00:03:39.080
on their research projects.

87
00:03:39.080 --> 00:03:41.780
These relationships can
also be depicted in

88
00:03:41.780 --> 00:03:43.370
an ER diagram as

89
00:03:43.370 --> 00:03:46.495
many students are
supervised by many staff.

90
00:03:46.495 --> 00:03:48.500
You should now be
able to outline

91
00:03:48.500 --> 00:03:50.120
the different
relationships that exist

92
00:03:50.120 --> 00:03:54.690
between tables in a relational
database model. Good work.